if (((OMF_data *)((OMF_record *)tmp)->content)->seg_idx - 1 >= obj->nb_section) {
    eprintf ("Invalid Ledata record (bad segment index)\n");
    return false;
}
OMF_segment *os = obj->sections[((OMF_data *)((OMF_record *)tmp)->content)->seg_idx - 1];
if (os && (tmp_data = os->data)) {
    ...
} else {
    obj->sections[((OMF_data *)((OMF_record *)tmp)->content)->seg_idx - 1]->data = ((OMF_record *)tmp)->content;
}
```

If `obj->nb_section == 0` (no SEGDEF records), then `obj->sections` is NULL. The check `seg_idx - 1 >= 0` is true for any `seg_idx >= 1`, so the function returns false. But what if `seg_idx == 0`? Then `0 - 1 = 65535 >= 0` is true, so it returns false. Good.

But what if `obj->nb_section > 0` and `seg_idx` is valid, but `obj->sections[seg_idx - 1]` is NULL? This could happen if `get_omf_section_info` didn't set all section pointers. But we established that this shouldn't happen.

Wait, what if `get_omf_section_info` is called but there are no SEGDEF records? Then `obj->nb_section = 0`, and `obj->sections` is not allocated. But `get_omf_data_info` is still called, and if there are LEDATA records, it would try to access `obj->sections[...]` which is NULL.

But the check `seg_idx - 1 >= obj->nb_section` would catch this: `seg_idx - 1 >= 0` is true for any `seg_idx >= 1`, so it returns false. And if `seg_idx == 0`, `0 - 1 = 65535 >= 0` is true, so it returns false.

So this is safe. But what if `seg_idx` is 0 and `obj->nb_section` is 0? Then `0 - 1 = 65535 >= 0` is true, so it returns false. Good.

